<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Backup / transfer policy for Android 12+ (API 31), referenced from
    android:dataExtractionRules in the manifest.

    android:allowBackup is "true" (it was "false" from the first release until the
    hidden-words store shipped). What that flag never governed, from Android 12, is device-
    to-device transfer: per the Auto Backup docs, "on devices from some device manufacturers,
    specifying android:allowBackup='false' disables cloud-based backup and restore ... but
    doesn't disable device-to-device transfers for the app." So these rules, not the flag,
    are the policy on every channel, and they are written to be safe under either flag value.

    ONE directory rides both channels: files/vocab/ (HiddenWordsStore.BACKUP_DIR), which
    holds the hidden-words store, files/vocab/hidden_words.sqlite, and, only between a crash
    mid-write and the store's next open, that store's SQLite rollback journal. The rule
    names the DIRECTORY on purpose: a copy of the main file alone, taken while a hot journal
    exists, would restore as a half-applied write with nothing to roll it back, whereas the
    two files together restore as exactly the post-crash state SQLite is built to recover
    from (the same pair the platform's database domain copies for every app). Nothing else
    may be placed in vocab/. The store is user-curated primary data expected to reach
    thousands of rows, with no other way to survive a reinstall or a new device until a
    management screen with export exists. It is a vocabulary list: not secret, and far
    weaker as a record of what the user reads than the translation-history database, which
    stays excluded. BackupRulesTest pins the include to the store's constants and the rest
    of this file's shape.

    Everything else stays out of every channel, as before: it is either secret (the API keys
    in shared_prefs/playtranslate_prefs.xml), private (the translation-history database), or
    bulky and re-downloadable (LLM models, dictionary packs, OCR models, which live under
    no_backup and are never eligible anyway).

    Belt and braces, on purpose. The presence of an <include> already makes backup
    include-only (the docs: "If you specify an <include> element, the system no longer
    includes any files by default"), so the excludes below are redundant while the include
    parses. They are kept so the API-key guard does not rest on that one semantic: if the
    include line were ever dropped or broken, the defaults would reopen every domain, and
    the explicit excludes would still hold the sharedpref and database domains closed.
    Under files/, the two other residents (vad/, the Silero VAD model copy; crashes/, crash
    reports) are excluded by name for the same reason.

    Two syntax traps worth knowing before editing:
      * `path` is REQUIRED on a rule. A rule with no path is silently SKIPPED, so
        `<exclude domain="root" />` would look like an opt-out and do nothing.
      * `path="."` means the domain's root directory, and a directory rule applies
        recursively to everything beneath it.

    The third section, <cross-platform-transfer> (Android-to-iOS, from Android 16 QPR2 /
    API 36.1), is deliberately absent, and ADDING IT WOULD BE A MISTAKE. It is not a hole.

    The two sections above are on by default — omit them and the mode is "fully enabled
    for all content" — which is why they exclude every domain exhaustively. Cross-platform
    transfer inverts that: the section is how an app *joins* the channel, not how it leaves.
    It requires platform="ios" plus a <platform-specific-params> child carrying the bundleId,
    teamId and contentVersion of a counterpart iOS app, and those IDs ARE the destination —
    per the Auto Backup docs they ensure "data is only transferred to the specified app on
    the other platform". PlayTranslate has no iOS build, so there is no counterpart to name,
    nowhere for data to go, and nothing to exclude. Note the shape of the argument: an app
    without an iOS twin cannot author a valid section here at all, so the mode cannot be
    on-by-default — the apps that would most need an opt-out could not express one.

    Writing the section anyway (with invented IDs, since we have no real ones) is the single
    action that would opt us in, and a missing required attribute makes this whole file fail
    to parse — which falls back to the DEFAULT rules and reopens cloud backup and D2D. So the
    "fix" costs more than the imaginary bug. If PlayTranslate ever ships on iOS, revisit with
    real IDs; until then, absence is the correct and safe configuration.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <include domain="file" path="vocab" />
        <exclude domain="file" path="vad" />
        <exclude domain="file" path="crashes" />
        <exclude domain="root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
        <exclude domain="external" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_sharedpref" path="." />
    </cloud-backup>

    <device-transfer>
        <include domain="file" path="vocab" />
        <exclude domain="file" path="vad" />
        <exclude domain="file" path="crashes" />
        <exclude domain="root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
        <exclude domain="external" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_sharedpref" path="." />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
